{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+yz)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xz)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(0)(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(1))\\text{ }j+(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(0))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(1,0,1)=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497419 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186497423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497419 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "053dd984-66f7-5e60-a777-6ca3631e9fa4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497419 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497423 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186497474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497423 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "14aa383b-ac4e-5a2e-b93c-5310c25c9ba2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497423 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186398954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497425 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186497443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497425 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "17889977-02c9-5363-abaf-c90dc6ba9c00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497425 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186265960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497443 #edb/ref