artesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-(-4))(0)-(y-(0))(-8) & =0 \\\\ 8 y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537098 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537092 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537098 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537098 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=7.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537098 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c33c871-3aa8-55d7-b3c4-b164edfa69d7" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537098 #edb/ref 1074 "If we set $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=7.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6 x)\\,i+(8 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =(6 (1))\\,i+(8 (-1))\\,j \\\\  & =(6)\\,i+(-8)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,-1)=⟨6,-8⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(1)}{6} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{-8} \\\\ (-8)(x-(1)) & =(6)(y-(-1)) \\\\ (-4)(x-(1)) & =(3)(y-(-1)) \\\\ 4 - 4 x & =3 y + 3 \\\\ 4 x + 3 y - 1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537099 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537116 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537099 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537099 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $x^{2} + 2 y^{2}=9.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537099 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3df1a962-28ed-5ee8-927b-1c62b88a956c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537099 #edb/ref 1074 "If we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=9.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,-2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x)\\,i+(4 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-2) & =(2 (1))\\,i+(4 (-2))\\,j \\\\  & =(2)\\,i+(-8)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨2,-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,-2)=⟨2,-8⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-2).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(1)}{2} & =\\frac{y-(-2)}{-8} \\\\ (-8)(x-(1)) & =(2)(y-(-2)) \\\\ (-4)(x-(1)) & =(1)(y-(-2)) \\\\ 4 - 4 x & =y + 2 \\\\ 4 x + y - 2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537100 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537096 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537100 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537100 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $2 x^{2} + y^{2}=17.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-2,-3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537100 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3df7d364-776e-5dbb-9f2e-436f4e9fc804" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537100 #edb/ref 1074 "If we set $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=17.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-2,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-2,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{2} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(4 x)\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3) & =(4 (-2))\\,i+(2 (-3))\\,j \\\\  & =(-8)\\,i+(-6)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-8,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-2,-3)=⟨-8,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-2,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-2)}{-8} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-6} \\\\ (-6)(x-(-2)) & =(-8)(y-(-3)) \\\\ (-3)(x-(-2)) & =(-4)(y-(-3)) \\\\ - 3 x - 6 & =- 4 y - 12 \\\\ 3 x - 4 y - 6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537085 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537113 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537113 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $3 x^{2} + y^{2}=36.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,-3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537113 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a06355a9-e2d0-5a7e-bed1-e1b39de7ef4e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537113 #edb/ref 1074 "If we set $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=36.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x^{2} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x^{2} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6 x)\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,-3) & =(6 (3))\\,i+(2 (-3))\\,j \\\\  & =(18)\\,i+(-6)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨18,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,-3)=⟨18,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(3)}{18} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-6} \\\\ (-6)(x-(3)) & =(18)(y-(-3)) \\\\ (-1)(x-(3)) & =(3)(y-(-3)) \\\\ 3 - x & =3 y + 9 \\\\ x + 3 y + 6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537122 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537114 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537122 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537122 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}=22.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537122 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c34c8b85-1410-5a9f-8287-4a3353c69bd2" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537122 #edb/ref 1074 "If we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=22.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(8 x)\\,i+(4 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,3) & =(8 (-1))\\,i+(4 (3))\\,j \\\\  & =(-8)\\,i+(12)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-8,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,3)=⟨-8,12⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{-8} & =\\frac{y-(3)}{12} \\\\ (12)(x-(-1)) & =(-8)(y-(3)) \\\\ (3)(x-(-1)) & =(-2)(y-(3)) \\\\ 3 x + 3 & =6 - 2 y \\\\ 3 x + 2 y - 3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537130 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537131 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537130 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537130 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2}=1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-3,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537130 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa107303-abd7-5cc9-bd95-cc8524938adb" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537130 #edb/ref 1074 "If we set $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-3,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-3,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-3,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2 x}{9})\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-3,0) & =(\\frac{2 (-3)}{9})\\,i+(2 (0))\\,j \\\\  & =(- \\frac{2}{3})\\,i+(0)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{2}{3},0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-3,0)=⟨- \\frac{2}{3},0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-3,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-(-3))(0)-(y-(0))(- \\frac{2}{3}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2 y}{3} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537133 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186124462.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537133 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "0b2d95c9-6d9e-5b87-bc06-9168b9223277" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186544099 #edb/ref 1080 "2x+3y-2=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544099 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544099 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "058f601c-efb8-52f4-ad80-c86475d38425" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544366 #edb/ref 1080 "x-y-1=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544366 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544366 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7f6d7d30-ea91-5f19-ad12-6c285ca3401e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544512 #edb/ref 1080 "x+y-1=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544512 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544512 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ca4a37f5-406e-5615-bae5-8b7a2a141fbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544542 #edb/ref 1080 "x+2y-1=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544542 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544542 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d617b5ac-9818-5c3b-ba03-76a6cc05aeca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544576 #edb/ref 1080 "x-2y-1=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544576 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544576 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e3119854-8196-5825-a5c9-16cfb48b0d16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544726 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544726 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544726 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186544542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544726 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9ced3481-0c5c-5eca-982f-047cab2301fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544815 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544815 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544815 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544815 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544815 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e15364c-186a-5328-8307-13438fe58309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544815 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2x)i+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)+2y)j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(2(1)(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))-2(1))i+((1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))+2(0))j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+j \\\\  & =⟨-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-2,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-2} & =\\frac{y-0}{1} \\\\ x-1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567422 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186567423 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567422 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567422 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(3,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567422 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "161f4c79-57b1-5774-afbb-2d7aca169df7" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567422 #edb/ref 1074 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point  where $(x,y)=(3,1)$, can be found by computing $∇g(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(3,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\,i+\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x})\\,i+(\\frac{1}{y})\\,j \\\\ ∇g(3,1) & =(\\frac{1}{(3)})\\,i+(\\frac{1}{(1)})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇g(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇g(3,1)}{‖∇g(3,1)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j}{\\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{3})^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}(i+3\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567427 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186567428 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567427 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567427 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(9,16).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567427 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33daf655-8020-5d37-af88-f1b07a868f33" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567427 #edb/ref 1074 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(9,16)$ can be found by computing $∇f(9,16).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(9,16):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})\\,i+(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(9,16) & =(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{9}})\\,i+(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,i+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(9,16):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(9,16)}{‖∇f(9,16)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j)}{‖\\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(3)^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(2\\,i+3\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567444 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186567424 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567444 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567444 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(13,5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567444 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3e908d9-bc89-55b4-a12b-976025ac1c38" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567444 #edb/ref 1074 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(13,5)$, can be found by computing $∇f(13,5).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(13,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})\\,i+(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(13,5) & =(\\frac{(13)}{\\sqrt{(13)^{2}-(5)^{2}}})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +(-\\frac{(5)}{\\sqrt{(13)^{2}-(5)^{2}}})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{144}}\\,i-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{144}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{12}\\,i-\\frac{5}{12}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(13,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(13,5)}{‖∇f(13,5)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{13}{12}\\,i-\\frac{5}{12}\\,j}{\\sqrt{(\\frac{13}{12})^{2}+(-\\frac{5}{12})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{13}{12}\\,i-\\frac{5}{12}\\,j}{\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{194}(13\\,i-5\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567447 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186567448 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567447 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567447 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567447 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b488d0d8-0d5c-58db-a90e-48c42f1d42c6" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567447 #edb/ref 1074 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,0)$, can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})\\,i+(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(\\frac{(1)}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}-(0)^{2}}})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +(-\\frac{(0)}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}-(0)^{2}}})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}\\,i-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1}}\\,j \\\\  & =i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,0)}{‖∇f(1,0)‖} & =\\frac{i}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{i}{1} \\\\  & =i\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567450 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186567451 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567450 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567450 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(4,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567450 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db955fef-1148-5a1c-a192-f4c8263ee71a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567450 #edb/ref 1074 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(4,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(4,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}))\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})}{1+{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{1+{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,i+(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(4,0) & =(-\\frac{(0)}{(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +(\\frac{(4)}{(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{16}\\,i+\\frac{4}{16}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(4,0)}{‖∇f(4,0)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}(j)}{‖\\frac{1}{4}(j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}(j) \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567456 #edb/ref 1168 "generators/dd/ddfd17b331d055e28eb1d1807a4d66f97df4b6564097788929a12efb91ba84d6/generator.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186567456 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "41f14332-6c49-59dc-980c-73071252df54" #edb/ref 17592186045443]}
